# Derived run reports — regenerated by their own script on every --write pass.
# Never a source of truth: read the committed assets/data.js instead.
engine/rollforward_ledger.json
engine/technicals_report.json

# Bytecode. THE SEPARATE DECISION THIS NOTE LEFT OPEN WAS MADE 07-09-2026 AND SIX
# .pyc FILES WERE UNTRACKED. The note was right that .gitignore does not untrack
# what is already tracked, and right to leave the choice rather than take it in
# passing — what forced it is that [R-ENF-01 EXTENDED 07-09-2026]'s tree-unmodified
# gate asks whether running the checks CHANGED the tree, and a .pyc header embeds
# the source's mtime, which on a fresh checkout is the checkout time. So every CI
# run recompiled three of them and the gate refused, correctly: THE TRACKED TREE
# DID CHANGE. Leaving them tracked meant a permanently red check, which [R-ENF-02]
# forbids, and teaching the gate to ignore .pyc would have suppressed a true
# report. Nothing reads them — every reference to __pycache__ in this repository
# is an EXCLUSION in a sandbox or a walker — and all six sources are present, so
# they are pure caches. They were last re-committed by the UNATTENDED PIPELINE,
# whose `git add -A` swept them back in because a tracked file ignores .gitignore;
# untracking stops that at the source. The rules below now bind on them.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# scripts/check_ta_chart_overlay.js — the MANDATORY overlay gate — needs playwright,
# and there is no package.json, so running it means `npm install --no-save playwright`
# in the repo root. That leaves ~100MB of untracked noise in `git status` on every
# pass that honours the gate. The browser binary itself is pre-provisioned at
# /opt/pw-browsers/chromium (the script hardcodes that executablePath), so only the
# node package is ever fetched.
node_modules/

# Phase C point-in-time fair-value cache (derived from git history; self-invalidating)
engine/.pit_fair_values_*.json
engine/.pit_tmp_data.js

# Local virtualenv created to run the engine (numpy/pandas/scipy/openpyxl/python-docx).
# Build tooling, never a source of truth.
.venv/
# AIRARABIA study source documents (62MB of primary PDFs from airarabia.com IR +
# their local OCR text). Provenance lives in the study's bibliography document;
# the PDFs themselves are retrievable from the URLs recorded there.
engine/airarabia_study/sources/
# BOROUGE primary source PDFs — 78 MB of company filings. Excluded deliberately, NOT
# because they are unimportant but because the provenance chain does not depend on
# storing them: engine/borouge_study/source_access.json carries the public URL, the byte
# count and the SHA-256 of all 21, and verify_sources.py re-downloads and re-hashes every
# one. A hash plus a public URL proves more than a committed binary does. The extracted
# .txt the build actually reads IS committed. See engine/borouge_study/src/README.md.
engine/borouge_study/src/*.pdf

# Day-0 refetch: primary documents re-fetched and sha256-verified against the walk-forward registers stay on disk for the session; the verified url+sha record is engine/method_reassessment/refetch_log.json. The base-year and latest-interim statements each study stands on are committed under the study directories explicitly.
engine/*_walkforward/filings/

# The framework build board is GENERATED and deliberately NOT committed: GitHub Pages
# serves this whole tree, so an .html file here is served on the live site, and this
# page carries internal vocabulary — gates, ratchets, held studies. Committed: the
# generator (build_board.py) and its state (board_state.json). Rendered: to a scratch
# path, and published from there as an artefact.
engine/method_reassessment/board.html

# session tokens and fetch scratch — never committed, the repository is public
**/.cookies
**/.rc.html
**/text/.ocrtmp-*.png

# OCR page scratch and bytecode — regenerable, never committed
**/text/.pg-*.png
**/.pg-*.png
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

# Regenerable run artefacts. THE TEST IS NOT "is it generated" BUT "can it be
# rebuilt from what is committed" — fv_vintages.json, escalations.json and the
# ledger are generated too and must NEVER be listed here, because they record
# what a number was on the day it was published and nothing can reconstruct
# that afterwards [R-VCAL-01].
#
# ocr/*.png        page images rendered from the PDFs already committed under
#                  each study's src/pdf/. ~49MB across two names; cheap to
#                  regenerate, permanent in every future clone if committed.
# ci_gate_run.json a recorded gate run is evidence about THE TREE IT RAN ON
#                  [R-ENF-06], and every commit invalidates it — a committed
#                  one is a claim about a tree that no longer exists.
# progress.json    written by method_reassessment/progress.py --json, read by
#                  nothing, and stamps `generated` with the current time, so it
#                  reads as modified after every run even when nothing moved.
engine/*_study_pending/**/ocr/*.png
engine/build_depth_audit/ci_gate_run.json
engine/method_reassessment/progress.json

# Saved copies of third-party web pages captured as study source (an IR site as it
# stood on a date). ~4MB each; the research value is the extracted text committed
# beside them as txt_*.txt. They are NOT pages this site serves, and committing them
# made check_page_integrity apply our own page-chrome rules to somebody else's HTML.
engine/*_study_pending/**/*.html

# QC-AUDIT SCRATCH. An auditor working from outside a study renders its PDF pages to
# images, extracts text and takes backups, and it writes them where it is standing --
# the repository root. On 17-09-2026 a `git add -A` in this session swept 37 of them
# into a commit whose real content was one markdown file, WHILE THE AUDIT WAS STILL
# RUNNING. The audit caught it; nothing else would have, because the files are
# individually innocuous and the commit message was about something else.
# THE LESSON IS ABOUT `git add -A` RATHER THAN ABOUT AUDITS: a blanket add stages
# whatever else happens to be in the tree, and a session with a concurrent agent
# always has something else in the tree.
.qc_*
